! jb_af1c F212-X "ADD TO FRACTION ROUTINE", sub-block F212XC "TEST
! UNCHANGED REGS", pp.123-124 (octal 016764.00-017005.40, the block's
! own exit "BD,$+1.0").  This completes F212-X.
!
! Neither sub-test looks at the arithmetic at all.  Each sets the
! whole accumulator and sign byte to a known value, runs one F+, and
! asks that the parts F+ must NOT disturb came through unchanged:
!
!   XC1  Z,$L / Z,$R / Z,$SB -- everything zero -- then F+(U),FPK21.
!        "R 44-63, SB 0-3 + 5-7 REM 0".
!   XC2  C1111 both halves and CM1111 the sign byte -- everything one
!        -- then F+(U),FPK37.  "R 44-63, SB 0-3 + 5-7 REM 1".
!
! R 44-63 is word 9 bits 44-63, which is IBM 108-127: the twenty bits
! below the 96-bit fraction, which fpdAccRight carries through from
! the old right half.  SB 0-3 and 5-7 are the sign byte with BIT 4
! DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, because bit 4 is the result sign and F+ does
! rewrite it; 0-3 and 5-7 (the three data flags among them) must not
! move.
!
! XC1 IS THE ONE THAT DISCRIMINATES, and the listing flags why in its
! own comment on the F+ row: "MEM FLAGS+7".  FPK21's flag bits are
! 111, so an implementation that took the sign byte's flags from the
! ADDRESSED OPERAND rather than from the accumulator would leave SB
! 5-7 at 7 against an accumulator zeroed a moment earlier, and the
! "DOES SB5-7 REMAIN+0-S" check would catch it.  The model takes them
! from accS -- sbWith(accS, sign, sbFlags(accS)) -- for the reason
! F208C2 pinned on D+, and this is the row that tests it from the
! other side.
!
! Note what XC2's accumulator is: all ones means the exponent field is
! all ones too, so the accumulator is XFN and F+ performs no addition.
! XC1's is all zeros, an N-range accumulator, so there it does.  The
! two sub-tests therefore check the preservation on BOTH of F+'s
! paths, which is more than the wording suggests.
!
! The connectives read as "result must be all zeros, or BRGZ fires":
! CT0101/CT1010 test the ACCUMULATOR field (FPK0/FPK1 are the dummy
! storage operand), CT0011/CT1100 test the MEMORY field at $SB.
!
! TRANSCRIPTION NOTE: the page prints the SIC after XC2's CT1010 at
! 017000.00, but CT1010 is a full word starting at 016777.40 and so
! occupies 017000.00 itself; the BRGZ that pairs with that SIC is
! printed at 017001.00, which puts the SIC at 017000.40.  Taken as
! 017000.40 -- the derivation and the following row agree, and only
! the one printed address does not.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)22005.0
FPK0    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000000
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)22032.0
FPK21   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0031177777777777777767
        SLC, (8)22052.0
FPK37   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0031125252525252525240

        SLC, (8)16764.0
FAF4    Z, $L                                ! F212XC1 - R 44-63, SB 0-3 + 5-7 rem 0
        Z, $R
        Z, $SB
        F+(U), FPK21                         ! mem flags + 7
        CT0101(BU,20), FPK0                  ! does R 44-63 remain +0-s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,4), $SB                    ! does SB0-3 remain +0-s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,3), $SB+.5                 ! does SB5-7 remain +0-s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1, 64               ! F212XC2 - R 44-63, SB 0-3 + 5-7 rem 1
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1
        CM1111(BU,8), $SB
        F+(U), FPK37
        CT1010(BU,20), FPK1                  ! does R44-63 remain +1-s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,4), $SB                    ! does SB0-3 remain +1-s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,3), $SB+.5                 ! does SB 5-7 remain +1-s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                               ! 017005.40: the block's exit "BD,$+1.0"
        END, FAF4
